  "question": {
    "text": "On a temperature scale &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi></math>&#39;, the boiling point of water is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>65</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> and the freezing point is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math>. Assuming that the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi></math>&nbsp;scale is linear. The equivalent temperature corresponding to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>95</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> on the Fahrenheit scale would be"
  },
